                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="842-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_hc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Hypercardioid" title="Hypercardioid" ></span>Neumann KM 185</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Hypercardioid Pencil Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The KM 185 is a hypercardioid FET pencil condenser. It is the newest addition to the &#8220;Series 180&#8221; microphones, which includes the well-known KM 184 cardioid and KM 183 omni microphones.</p><p>Note that the capsules in the Series 180 mics are <em>not</em> interchangeable.</p><p>The capsule in the KM 185 is Neumann&#8217;s model <b>K 50</b>, developed in 1988. Is a pressure-gradient transducer with a gold-sputtered Mylar diaphragm. Internally, the K 50 capsule is identical to Neumann&#8217;s AK 50 capsule. The AK 50 is a detachable hypercardioid capsule that is part of the KM 100 Series of microphones.</p><p>The KM185 is available in matte black (model KM 185 mt, p/n 008441) or satin nickel (p/n 008440) finishes. The mic ships with a foam windscreen (p/n WNS 100) and a black swivel mic clip (p/n SG 21 bk). Matched stereo pairs are also available, in either color.</p><blockquote><p><cite>MIX, 2000</cite></p><p>The KM 185 hypercardioid worked wonderfully on the hi-hat&#8230; Because of its pattern, I could put the mic a little farther away&#8230; which sounds better than putting the mic extra close to minimize leakage.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Neumann/KM-185">Neumann KM 185</a> is also known as: KM185.</p><p>The mic was released in 1999.</p>
